cartridge "BOOMERANG/COMPUTE'S GAZETTE/1989"

' BOOMERANG
' A ColecoVision game based on "Boomerang", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk,
' August 1989 (Commodore 64 machine language, BOOMERANG, SYS6365; no
' author is named in the program or on the disk). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/code.dis and orig/main.dis, and watched in jsc64,
' orig/c64-*.png), the game is built for the ColecoVision: grass and
' stone tiles, smooth sprites, spinning boomerangs, sound effects.
'
' The game: Blue and Red stand at the two ends of a walled field. Each
' has one boomerang. Press the button to throw it the way you last moved.
' It flies straight, twice as fast as you walk, until it hits a wall;
' there it drops and lies. While it is in the air and within 20 columns
' and 14 rows of you, the button sends it off again the way you are now
' facing: steer it round the maze. Walk onto your own boomerang (in the
' air or lying) to catch it. The other one's boomerang, flying or lying
' on the ground, knocks you out. Each hit costs a life; the counter
' starts at 9 and a hit at 0 eliminates you. After each hit both start
' again at their ends, boomerangs in hand ("PREPARE FOR BATTLE!").
'
' From the original (orig/): the field is a frame with a random maze laid
' on a grid of 4 x 4 cells: on every grid line each segment is a wall 1
' time in 4 (the segments at the ends of a line 1 time in 8); MAZE NO
' leaves the field empty; each player may refuse the maze and have
' another drawn; the walking steps (4 C64 pixels across or 2 down per
' pass of the main loop: across is twice as fast), the boomerang's two
' steps a pass, the speed 1-3 delay loop ($20/$10/$08 x 256 x 5 cycles,
' about 21, 39 and 67 passes a second with the rest of the loop), facing
' set by every push even against a wall, holding the button rethrowing at
' once, the redirect range (+-20 columns, +-14 rows), a lying boomerang
' still deadly to the other player, a hit on both in one pass counting
' for both, blue's elimination checked first, 10 lives shown as 9 to 0.
'
' Liberties: the field is 26 x 18 cells (the C64's 38 x 22 does not fit
' the TV-safe area), so the maze grid has 6 x 4 lines, not 9 x 5; motion
' is smooth (sub-pixel steps every frame at the C64's rates for speeds 1
' and 2; speed 3 is eased to 4/5 of the C64's 67 passes a second);
' touches are box tests (the C64 used its sprite collision register and a
' box); a thrown boomerang can be caught only after it has once left its
' thrower or lies (on the C64 it flew clear before the next collision
' check); a
' turn click when a boomerang is sent a new way; ONE PLAYER mode with a
' computer Red (the original was for two players only): it looks every 16
' frames, finds its way through the maze's rooms, takes a clear throw or a
' steer only every other look, and does not dodge; the game ends with
' the winner's message and goes back to the title (no PLAY AGAIN prompt,
' no Q to quit, no pause); a maze that walls Blue's end off from Red's
' is drawn again (on the C64 such a duel could never end unless a player
' refused the maze); a title page, a credits page and a title tune.
'
' Controls: controller 1 is Blue, controller 2 Red. Stick: walk (and
' face); either button: throw, or send your flying boomerang the way you
' face. Title: up/down choose a line, left/right or a button change it, a
' button on START begins. Maze check: stick toggles YES/NO, button
' answers.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects
' after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always
' given. Own xorshift random numbers.

